    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Over Vladimir Putin’s twenty-four years in office, he has increasingly promoted a united national mythology in the name of authoritarian regime security. His national mythology seems to have both strengthened his regime and entrenched anti-Ukrainian sentiments within the Russian Federation. Following the full-scale invasion in 2022, the reframing of history deepened, infiltrating university courses and school textbooks as well as state media. The existence of a national mythology is not in itself remarkable: all states need a national mythology because a founding narrative is a cornerstone of national identity. The question, however, is why has Russia’s mythology changed so much under Putin’s regime, and why has the mythology become so deeply state controlled? By analyzing changes over time in Putin’s public speeches and articles, it becomes clear that the national mythology currently present within Russia is not necessarily native but rather intentionally crafted by the Kremlin.  This is evident in the Kremlin’s consolidation of national identity and memory within Russia paired with increasing restrictions on civil liberties. Additionally, education has increasingly played a role in presenting the state’s historical narrative to youth populations. The fact that Putin is able to enact these reforms and yet maintain reportedly high approval ratings proves the efficacy of his slow-burn authoritarian consolidation. As the policies of past authoritarians have inspired Putin, so, too, will the success of his policies serve as an example for future authoritarians. In a time when far-right politics are on the rise globally, this is cause for great concern and should be watched carefully, and attempts to emulate Putin must be called out and resisted.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Harvey, A. (2024). Building Putinism: National Mythology and Education in Russia. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/qc560nj2846. https://doi.org/10.25740/qc560nj2846.",
            "type": "preferred citation",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        }
